🚽 Test case: the logical NOT operator.
🚽
🚽 `!` had no token at all. lang.l's catch-all rule silently discarded any
🚽 unrecognized character, so `!a` lexed as plain `a` and every negation
🚽 evaluated to its own operand: `!L` was L, and `edgy (!done)` ran the
🚽 wrong branch, with no diagnostic anywhere.
🚽
🚽 Covers every operand type, because `!` cannot borrow the caller's
🚽 context type the way `-` can. A chad must be judged in float precision
🚽 (0.5 is true, so !0.5 is L -- truncating to int would call it false),
🚽 and reading a rizz back through a bool is a type-punned load that UBSan
🚽 rejects outright.
rizz takes_int(rizz x) { bussin x + 1; }
chad takes_float(chad x) { bussin x * 2.0; }
smol takes_short(smol x) { bussin x; }

skibidi main {
    cap a = L;
    rizz n = 5;
    rizz z = 0;
    chad f = 0.5;
    gigachad d = 0.0;
    smol s = 3;

    yapping("%b %b", !a, !W);
    yapping("%b %b", !n, !z);
    yapping("%b", !f);
    yapping("%b", !d);
    yapping("%b", !s);

    🚽 A truth value, never a pointer: `!p` is the null check it looks like.
    🚽 Reporting pointer level 1 here made callers evaluate it as an address.
    rizz *p = &n;
    yapping("%b", !p);

    🚽 Same precedence as unary minus, as in C: these are (!a) == W,
    🚽 (!a) && (!W), and !(!a) -- not negations of the whole expression.
    yapping("%b %b %b", !a == W, !a && !W, !!a);

    🚽 A smol is stored packed at sizeof(short), so judging it through
    🚽 the int evaluator reads four bytes out of a two-byte slot. This
    🚽 line answered L before expression_is_truthy() learned to dispatch
    🚽 VAR_SHORT: arr[0] is 0, but the load pulled in arr[1] and saw
    🚽 0x00010000. Both the array and the pointer form are covered because
    🚽 they reach the packed storage by different routes -- a scalar smol
    🚽 sits in zeroed union storage and passes either way, which is what
    🚽 made this look fine.
    smol packed[2] = {0, 1};
    yapping("%b", !packed[0]);
    smol *sp = &packed[0];
    yapping("%b", !(*sp));

    🚽 `!` yields cap, and cap does not silently become rizz here
    🚽 (`rizz k = W;` is a type error too -- see
    🚽 logical_not_cap_to_rizz_fail). Untyped integer contexts still get
    🚽 C's 0/1: an array index and a switch selector below.
    🚽
    🚽 The three calls after them are NOT a checked conversion -- this
    🚽 analyzer does not type-check user-defined call arguments against
    🚽 their parameters. They are here because the runtime evaluates each
    🚽 argument in the PARAMETER's type, which is the only way to reach
    🚽 the chad, gigachad and smol OP_NOT intercepts at all.
    rizz arr[2] = {7, 9};
    yapping("%d", arr[!z]);

    ohio (!a) {
        sigma rule 1:
            yapping("switch: one");
            bruh;
        based:
            yapping("switch: default");
    }

    yapping("%d", takes_int(!z));
    yapping("%.1f", takes_float(!z));
    yapping("%d", takes_short(!z));

    🚽 The shape the bug actually broke in real code.
    cap done = L;
    rizz i = 0;
    goon (!done) {
        i = i + 1;
        edgy (i >= 3) { done = W; }
    }
    yapping("%d", i);

    🚽 `!=` must still lex as one token, not `!` followed by `=`.
    edgy (n != z) { yapping("ne ok"); }

    bussin 0;
}
